WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:07.507
Water is deadlier than wind
when it comes to hurricanes.

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:10.276
In fact, it causes 90%
of the loss of lives

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:12.746
related to powerful storms.

00:00:12.746 --> 00:00:15.348
But when meteorologists talk
about a Category 3 system

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:17.450
headed their way, that classification

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.786
just takes wind speed into account.

00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:21.855
There's no single scale to indicate

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:25.058
both how strong and how
wet the systems are.

00:00:25.058 --> 00:00:29.662
In general, we tell people in
the meteorological community

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:34.234
to hide from the wind,
but run from the water.

00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:36.603
But if we know water can
actually cause more damage,

00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:39.539
why haven't we updated the
way we measure hurricanes?

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:41.408
Let's take a look back.

00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:44.277
The Saffir-Simpson
scale, going from 1 to 5,

00:00:44.277 --> 00:00:47.580
measures the speed of
sustained winds of a hurricane.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.749
It was developed in the 1970s to calculate

00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:52.519
how much wind can impact property.

00:00:52.519 --> 00:00:54.621
A storm becomes a Category 1 hurricane

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:57.290
when winds reach 74 miles per hour.

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:59.025
This kind of speed can damage roofs,

00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:01.928
sidings, and knock large
branches out of trees.

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:04.397
Major hurricanes start at Category 3

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:07.300
once winds increase to 111 miles per hour.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.968
They can cause devastating destruction

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:10.537
to well-constructed buildings.

00:01:10.537 --> 00:01:12.772
And there's a high chance
of injury or death.

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:18.078
Category 5 hurricanes have
winds over 157 miles per hour,

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:20.013
and are the top of the scale.

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:22.282
These systems can devastate large areas,

00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:24.317
leaving them uninhabitable for months.

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:27.520
But again, this is just from the winds.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:28.888
It doesn't take into consideration

00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:31.257
the impacts of storm surge and rain.

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:34.327
Storm surge happens when
winds push water from the sea

00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:36.996
onto the land, flooding large areas.

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:40.300
It's fast, powerful, and can
send 10 to 20 feet of water

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.635
several miles inland from the coast.

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:45.238
Hurricane Katrina was one
of the deadliest storms

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:47.607
to ever hit the mainland United States.

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:50.143
But its 127 miles per hour winds

00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:52.812
meant it was only
classified as a Category 3

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:55.014
when it made landfall in Louisiana.

00:01:55.014 --> 00:01:57.383
The water broke the levies
protecting New Orleans.

00:01:57.383 --> 00:02:00.420
And within a day, about 80%
of the city was underwater.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.755
With Hurricane Katrina in 2005,

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:06.025
we really didn't, we didn't
have in the collective

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:10.997
public mindset the difference
between a really large

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:15.268
powerful storm and a
compact powerful storm.

00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:17.937
The pictures and the memories of Katrina

00:02:17.937 --> 00:02:19.572
aren't really about the wind damage.

00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:21.441
It's mostly about the storm surge,

00:02:21.441 --> 00:02:24.444
the levies failing in New
Orleans, things like that.

00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:25.979
And even smaller Category 1 systems

00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:29.349
like Hurricane Dolly, which
slammed South Texas in 2008,

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:32.886
can leave behind death and
destruction for the same reason.

00:02:32.886 --> 00:02:34.554
For a while, the Saffir-Simpson Scale

00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:36.589
also categorized storm surge,

00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:37.924
but it became difficult to communicate

00:02:37.924 --> 00:02:40.693
the specific dangers of
each storm to the public

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:43.863
since the threats from water
and wind can be so different.

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:47.300
So in 2017, NOAA's
National Hurricane Center

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:50.436
started issuing official storm
surge watches and warnings.

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:52.105
What's different about
the storm surge warning

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:53.806
is what's different about the storm surge.

00:02:53.806 --> 00:02:56.176
It can occur in somewhat
different locations

00:02:56.176 --> 00:02:59.179
than the strongest wind,
and at different times.

00:02:59.179 --> 00:03:01.414
These advisories are key
to helping people prepare,

00:03:01.414 --> 00:03:04.117
even if they're not directly
in the path of a storm.

00:03:04.117 --> 00:03:05.885
But they still leave
out potential flooding

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:07.720
that could be caused by rainfall,

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.323
which is a whole other advisory from NOAA.

00:03:10.323 --> 00:03:12.792
You need to know if your
location's going to be

00:03:12.792 --> 00:03:17.363
impacted by storm surge, or
potentially freshwater flooding

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:20.300
that occurs from incredible
rainfall amounts.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:24.204
Because the time to evacuate, or whatever,

00:03:24.204 --> 00:03:26.239
isn't when the water is
rising into your home,

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:28.708
or business. It's beforehand.

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:30.910
The Saffir-Simpson scale's
historical popularity

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.512
and basic 1-to-5 format

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:35.148
means it's unlikely to
go away anytime soon.

00:03:35.148 --> 00:03:37.317
But there are a few
alternatives out there,

00:03:37.317 --> 00:03:39.953
like the Hurricane Severity
Index, owned by StormGeo,

00:03:39.953 --> 00:03:42.021
a private weather intelligence company.

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:43.890
It uses a 50-point scale that measures

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:47.126
the size of a hurricane and
the intensity of its winds.

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:50.196
Each component gets 25
points, to be more precise.

00:03:50.196 --> 00:03:53.066
Size is very important because it matters

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:56.169
for the aerial extent of the impact,

00:03:56.169 --> 00:03:59.005
how much time a hurricane's
going to hit you.

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:01.207
Things like storm surge, wave heights,

00:04:01.207 --> 00:04:02.842
rain amounts are greater.

00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:05.011
The HSI can also help
people compare storms

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:06.779
they might have already experienced

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:09.048
to get an idea of what to expect.

00:04:09.048 --> 00:04:11.251
The index would have placed
Katrina's damage potential

00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:13.219
in line with a Category 5.

00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:16.055
While its intensity was only 13 out of 25,

00:04:16.055 --> 00:04:19.125
its size was a 23 out of 25.

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:21.661
But media outlets rarely
use the index as a tool,

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:23.997
relying mostly on the
Saffir-Simpson scale.

00:04:23.997 --> 00:04:26.432
Most journalists include
NOAA's storm surge advisories

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:28.034
in their reports as well.

00:04:28.034 --> 00:04:29.769
But most of the attention around a storm

00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:32.672
still seems to center on
the category it falls under.

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:35.408
Atlantic hurricane season goes from

00:04:35.408 --> 00:04:37.210
June 1 to November 30.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:39.812
And 2020 has been a
particularly active one.

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:43.049
With things like climate
change and a warming world,

00:04:43.049 --> 00:04:46.819
hurricanes are more, there's going to be

00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:49.255
more intense hurricanes making landfall.

00:04:49.255 --> 00:04:52.558
NOAA predicted 19 to 25 named storms.

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:56.729
And as of September 21,
we've already had 23.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:59.699
That's already beaten last
year's 18 named storms,

00:04:59.699 --> 00:05:01.267
which was also above average.

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:03.703
And even as weather forecasting has been

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:06.339
refined and improved, experts
say there's no substitute

00:05:06.339 --> 00:05:09.309
for always being ready for a storm to hit.

00:05:09.309 --> 00:05:11.177
We have to make sure everybody understands

00:05:11.177 --> 00:05:13.146
that the forecasts aren't perfect.

00:05:13.146 --> 00:05:16.115
And sometimes the storm does something

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:17.717
not quite what we forecast,

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:19.018
and you really need to be prepared.

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:20.987
Most importantly, follow the advice

00:05:20.987 --> 00:05:24.724
of your local emergency
management officials.

